Text that is not SuperCollider, and what the formatter does with it.

The formatter must never repair: what sclang refuses must not come out of
it as something sclang accepts. tests/lang11d.rs holds each form here to
what its heading says. The file is read as interpreted.forms is.

== sclang refuses it, and so does the formatter

x = a+.2b;

f = { |a|-1 };

f = { |a=-1| a };

f = { arg a=-1; a };

f = { || 1 };

f = { |a|/*c*/ a };

f = { |a, b|-1 };

f = { |a = 1|-1 };

f = { arg a b; a + b };

f = { arg a, b c = 2; a + b + c };

f = { arg a, b = 1 + ; a };

f = { arg a = 1 2; a };

f = { |a ! b| a };

f = { |a = 1 + b| a };

f = { arg a = 1 b = 2; a };

f = { |a ; b| a };

f = { |a @ b = 2| a };

var a=-1; a

== sclang refuses it; the formatter takes it and writes text sclang still refuses

x = a <- b;

x = -a; y = a.neg; z = - a; w = -a.abs;

x = f.(a: 1, b: 2); y = f.value(1, b: 2); z = Foo(1, a: 2); w = foo(bar: 1);

x = foo(1, bar : 2);

w = foo(bar: 1);

x = -/*c*/1;

== sclang ends the text at an open comment, string or symbol and accepts it; the formatter refuses it

x = 1; // c
y = 2 /* d

x = 1;
"open

x = 1;
'open
